Wasmer 7.3.0, built from the pinned tag in named variants. A variant is an
ordered list of patch sets applied to the tag, declared in
<variant>/variant.env; its checkout lives at work/src/wasmer/<variant>, its
cargo output at work/build/wasmer/<variant>, and its CLI is installed at
work/wasmer/<variant>/bin/wasmer, mirroring how services are laid out.
Every variant builds only the Cranelift compiler backend; ServiceCache does
not build Wasmer's BUSL-1.1-licensed Singlepass and LLVM backends.

- stock: no patches. A guest with no extension declaration must run under
  it unchanged, and it is the unpatched baseline for test.sh.

- fixes: fixes to upstream Wasmer that are not specific to ServiceCache.

- extensions: the import namespaces a guest can opt into — currently
  ictrobot_shm_v1, cross-instance shared memory — with none of the host
  machinery. It applies the fixes set and then every extension's set, so
  an extension guest runs standalone, needing nothing from the servicecache
  set. Each extension lives whole in extensions/<name>/ at the repository root:
  its specification, guest header, demo, smoke test and its own patch set.
  A package that declares extensions builds and smoke-tests under this CLI.

- servicecache: the runtime the host embeds ([patch.crates-io] in Cargo.toml
  points at its checkout). It applies the fixes and extensions sets and then
  its own, which makes a guest coroutine suspend on a blocking host wait
  instead of parking its OS thread, lets guest threads be resurrected on fresh
  threads in a forked child, rebuilds the selector after a fork, replaces a
  clone's listening socket, snapshots shared memory across the native fork
  so clones diverge, keeps host I/O from going through a runtime handle
  captured at construction, and lets the host wait on a guest futex in the
  kernel. Suspension is off unless the host enables it; the I/O changes
  apply always.

build.sh <variant> builds and installs a variant's CLI; setup.sh <variant>
only prepares its checkout; test.sh <variant> runs the unit tests of the
crates that go into the built CLIs — a superset of everything the patch
sets modify — so patches carry their tests and the tests actually run;
upstream's integration suites and the backends we do not build are left to
upstream's CI. The matching make targets are wasmer-<variant>,
test-wasmer-<variant> and clean-wasmer-<variant>.

The patch sets are developed as stacked branches of a checkout under
work/wasmer-dev — a set's branch contains the branches below it — and
written back with export-patches.sh. They are maintained for the sys
backend on Linux.
